answer:
To cancel a fetch request, create an AbortController, and assign its AbortSignal to the request's signal property. Then, call the controller's abort() method to cancel the request. 

Here's an example from the page excerpts [4]: 
```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();

const fetchButton = document.querySelector("#fetch");
fetchButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  try {
    console.log("Starting fetch");
    const response = await fetch("https://example.org/get", {
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    console.log(`Response: ${response.status}`);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(`Error: ${e}`);
  }
});

const cancelButton = document.querySelector("#cancel");
cancelButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
  controller.abort();
  console.log("Canceled fetch");
});
```

And here's an example of what happens if the request is aborted after the fetch() call has been fulfilled but before the response body has been read [5]: 
```javascript
async function get() {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const request = new Request("https://example.org/get", {
    signal: controller.signal,
  });

  const response = await fetch(request);
  controller.abort();
  // The next line will throw `AbortError`
  const text = await response.text();
  console.log(text);
}
```

To check whether the response was successful before reading it, you can check the `Response.ok` property, which returns `true` if the status is in the 200 range.

Here's an example from the page:
```javascript
async function getData() {
  const url = "https://example.org/products.json";
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Response status: ${response.status}`);
    }
    // …
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error.message);
  }
}
```
This code checks if the response is OK (200-299) before trying to read the response body. If it's not OK, it throws an error.

You can encode form data as the request body by passing a `URLSearchParams` object as the `body` option.

Here is an example:
```javascript
const params = new URLSearchParams();
params.append("username", "example");

const response = await fetch(`https://example.org/login?${params}`);
```
This is cited from [5].
